e. The solution says \"Impossible by column counting.\" We need to verify that indeed k≥4 cannot be achieved. Let's think.\n\nWe need to show that any configuration of n lines covering all points must have at most 3 sunny lines. Or equivalently, if there are at least 4 sunny lines, then we cannot cover all points with n lines.\n\nWe need to prove a bound: at most 3 sunny lines. Let's try to reason.\n\nGiven n lines covering N = n(n+1)/2 points. Suppose we have s sunny lines, and the rest n-s are non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). The non-sunny lines can each be of one of three families. The sunny lines can be any slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nWe need to show s ≤ 3. Let's try to prove by counting columns (vertical lines) or rows.\n\nObservation: The points are arranged in a triangular shape. For each a (x-coordinate) from 1 to n, there are points (a,b) for b = 1,...,n+1-a. So the number of points in column a is n+1-a.\n\nIf we have a sunny line, it can intersect each vertical line at most once (since lines intersect at most once). So a sunny line can cover at most one point in each column (i.e., for each a). Actually a line can intersect a vertical line x = a at exactly one point (if not vertical). So a sunny line can cover at most one point per column. Similarly, a horizontal line can cover at most one point per row (i.e., for each b). A slope -1 line can also intersect each column at most one point, but also each row at most one point.\n\nThus each non-sunny line can also cover at most one point per column (except vertical lines cover all points in a column). Wait, vertical lines cover all points in a column. So vertical lines are special: they can cover many points in a column. Horizontal lines cover many points in a row. Slope -1 lines cover many points along a diagonal.\n\nThus to maximize coverage, we would use vertical lines for many points. The sunny lines are limited in that they can cover at most one point per column. So if we have many sunny lines, we need m