nting argument more carefully.\n\nWe have n columns (a from 1 to n). Suppose we have k sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once (since it's not vertical). So each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines. The rest of the points in that column must be covered by vertical lines. So for each column a, we need at least max(0, height - k) vertical lines? Actually a vertical line covers the entire column, so if we have a vertical line at column a, it covers all points in that column, regardless of height. So we can either assign a vertical line to a column, covering all points, or we can try to cover points in that column using sunny lines, but we need at most k sunny lines to cover at most k points. So if height > k, we must assign a vertical line to that column. So columns with height > k must have a vertical line. The number of such columns is n - k (as we computed). So we need at least n - k vertical lines. Since we have exactly n - k vertical lines (total lines n, k sunny), we can assign vertical lines to exactly those columns. So that part is okay.\n\nNow consider the columns with height ≤ k (the remaining k columns). For each such column, we need to cover all points using sunny lines. Since we have k sunny lines, each column can have at most k points covered, which is okay because height ≤ k. However, we need to ensure that each point in those columns is covered by some sunny line, and each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign each point to a sunny line, ensuring that each sunny line gets at most one point per column. This is possible if we can assign each column's points to distinct sunny lines. Since each column has at most k points, we have k sunny lines, we can assign each point in a column to a distinct sunny line. So we can assign all points in each column to distinct sunny lines. However, we must also ensure that each sunny line's set of assigned points across columns are collinear 